 {:seon.task/name "a-shell-call-loses-no-output"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by Context 2026-11-12 04:23 (notes seed-pin-npe-0423/seed.log): a bin/test seed died in seon.fx/pin with a NullPointerException, because `git status --porcelain=v2 --branch` in a submodule exited 0 and its output held no `# branch.oid` line. The same command was clean a minute later, and the rerun seeded green. Load was about 5, with batch 96's builds running beside it. The parse's part is fixed: seon.fx/pin now says what git printed (jolt/tests-formdata). The cause is not known. One reading: seon.lib/sh runs Jolt's clojure.java.shell/sh on a fiber that copies the child's pipes while it waits for the exit as an event; if the exit is taken before the copy has drained stdout, the answer's :out is short or empty. A second: two git commands in one submodule at once, though git status prints its header even when the index is locked. To do: reproduce with many concurrent `git status` calls through seon.lib/sh under load, comparing each :out with git's own; if the copy loses output, fix it in Jolt's sh (Deep fixes), so the exit is answered only after both pipes reach their end."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-shell-call-answers-its-output-or-says-why-not"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-12 04:23 (Context, bin/test seon.agent-test in worktree nsrefuse, at load about 5 while other sessions built; log in the notes folder's seed-pin-npe-0423): the seed died with \"nil where a java.lang.CharSequence is required\" at seon.fx/pin <- seon.fx/inputs <- seon.system/seed-program! <- seon.main/-main. seon.fx/git! throws on a nonzero exit, then returns (str/trim (:out r)), so the NPE means git-run, through seon.lib/sh, answered exit 0 with a nil :out: the call ran, said it succeeded, and lost its output. The same command a minute later answered its text. Its cause is not known yet: a read of the child's stdout that ended before the child wrote (the shell runs on a fiber), or a missed end of stream under load. Correct, at seon.lib/sh, the one shell: a call answers its exit and its whole output as text (an empty string when the child wrote nothing), or a typed error naming what could not be read. Never exit 0 with a nil :out, so no caller has to guess. Then the sweep: every caller that reads :out of sh. Proof, red first: a child that writes its output after a delay, run many times at once, always answers its full text, and a read that cannot complete is an error, never nil. See docs/machine.md#processes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-shell-call-answers-its-output-or-says-why-not}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-process-the-binary-starts-runs-as-production"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by deps via tests: a test shard runs Datahike's per-restore log/trace through trove's default *log-fn*, which production turns off, so tests measure a path production does not run. What exists: a process's start is written in four partial copies. The binary: gc-sweepers! 0 and end-with! the parent in seon.main/-main (main.clj:170-172). Root, after -main: ignore-sigpipe!, trove/set-log-fn! nil unless :log?, catch-uncaught!, raise-open-files!, sweepers from config, max-heap-free-ratio! in seon.system/start! (system.clj:1748-1781). A test shard: gc-sweepers! 0 only (test/test_runner.clj:874). One test namespace: a hand patch trove/set-log-fn! nil at load (test/seon/procs_test.clj:15). Forked processes (agents, test children, clones, the fork server's standby) inherit the parent's log fn, SIGPIPE disposition and sweeper pool, each adding only its role's steps (heap-cap!, intr/install!, intr/forked!, pdb/after-fork!), so a fork needs nothing from this change. The fix is one fn, seon.lib/start-process!, called first by every process started from the binary: (1) gc-sweepers! 0; (2) end-with! the parent when it is not launchd; (3) ignore-sigpipe!; (4) Datahike/trove logging set as production sets it (off); (5) the uncaught handler pointed at the process's own sink. Callers: seon.main/-main (every binary mode) and test_runner/-main. Root's start! keeps only open files, sweepers from config, heap-free ratio, and :log? turning logging on for checks that ask. The order is the physics (sweepers before anything collects), stated once in the fn, not a hand list. Deletes: test_runner/-main's sweeper call, system/start!'s sigpipe and log-fn lines, procs_test's log-fn patch, and the dead :noise flood (agent_proc.clj:216-220, :313, :319; system.clj:877). Reach: bin/system start, bin/update, bin/test, bin/prove, bin/check, the standing system, worktree systems, test children by inheritance; shared machinery, so one real bin/prove end to end before it lands. Proof, red first: in a shard, trove's *log-fn* is nil and SIGPIPE is ignored; red today. Estimate: two reads of process state, ~10k ticks; no per-call cost. Lands alone, first (before the Flow standard)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-process-the-binary-starts-runs-as-production seon.system-test/the-sweeper-pool-is-fixed-before-anything-collects}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
